Lee Chang-dong believes in a god of cinema. But like any deity worthy of worship, the celebrated South Korean auteur's higher power can't simply be summoned when you feel you need it most.

&#8220;All through production, director Lee would talk to me and our assistant director about this movie god,&#8221; says Kim Ji-yong, the cinematographer on Lee's acclaimed seventh feature and buzzy Oscar contender, Possible Love . &#8220;He said the god would only appear after we had tried everything &#x2014; put all our ideas, all of our energy, into the film. Only then, the god might come down to show us something beautiful &#x2014; something we never expected or imagined.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4],[300,251]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Movies 'In Starland': How Ray Panthaki, in His Directing Debut, Cast Clarence Maclin in His First Lead Role and Made a Very Personal Film Built on Meritocracy Business Can Peter Rice Fix Netflix? New Content Boss Has a Daunting To-Do List

&#8220;He really believes this,&#8221; Kim adds. &#8220;He even told us stories about the handful of times in the past when the movie god appeared on his sets.&#8221;

The most recent visitation came nearly a decade ago, during the tail end of production on Lee's beguiling 2018 dramatic thriller, Burning .

In the film's violent, enigmatic climax, the young protagonist, Jong-su (Yoo Ah-in), meets his wealthy rival, Ben (chillingly inhabited by Steven Yeun), on a remote road outside Seoul. Jong-su approaches as if to chat, then plunges a knife into Ben's stomach. He shoves the body into Ben's silver Porsche, strips off his bloodied clothes, throws them inside and sets the car on fire. Within the complicated framing of the story, it's unclear whether this episode actually happened or was imagined.

&#8220;That scene is shot as one long, continuous take during sunset, and they prepared for it very carefully,&#8221; Kim says, recounting Lee's story. &#8220;And if you remember, just as [Jong-su] is walking back to his truck naked, with the burning car behind him, snow starts falling heavily.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#8220; This was the movie god, according to director Lee,&#8221; Kim adds. &#8220;They didn't plan for it to snow in that moment; they couldn't have. But it happened, and it's so cinematic.&#8221;

Within the Korean film industry, it's no exaggeration to say that Lee himself is regarded as something of a deity, or at least a national treasure.

Born in 1954 to an impoverished but educated family in the southern Korean city of Daegu, Lee began his working life as a high school teacher before establishing himself as a fiction writer. He won acclaim for stories delicately attuned to the agonies and ironies of Korea's tumultuous postwar period, an era of brutal political repression and breakneck economic development. His characters tended to be sympathetic, disadvantaged outsiders &#x2014; sensitive people placed in impossible predicaments by the wrenching forces of ideology and history.

In the early 1990s, filmmaker friends recruited him to write screenplays, and in 1997 he made his directorial debut with Green Fish , a social critique disguised as a noirish crime movie. The film featured the first performance from Song Kang-ho , who would later become a national treasure in his own right through films like Park Chan-wook's Joint Security Area and Bong Joon Ho's Memories of Murder and Parasite .

Earlier this year, Squid Game creator Hwang Dong-hyuk told me that witnessing his favorite novelist &#x2014; Lee &#x2014; suddenly become a filmmaker was the moment that made him want to pursue a life in cinema himself. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#8220;I idolized him, and his career pivot kind of gave me courage to go for it,&#8221; Hwang said. &#8220;And then when I actually watched [ Green Fish ], it was stunning &#x2014; like every film he has done since.&#8221;

Five films followed before Possible Love , each an emotionally searing and intellectually electrifying portrait of people pushed to society's margins. With Peppermint Candy (1999), Lee issued a devastating state-of-the-nation statement by telling a ruined man's life in reverse. Moving backward through two decades of political repression, state violence and economic upheaval, the film gradually reveals how an innocent, hopeful young man became both an instrument and a casualty of his country's corruption. In Oasis (2002), he portrayed an extraordinary romance between a damaged ex-convict and a woman with severe cerebral palsy, reveling in the desires and imaginative freedom that the world around them refuses to acknowledge. The film earned Lee the Venice Film Festival's best directing prize.

He returned five years later with Secret Sunshine (2007), which follows a bereaved mother through a crisis so harrowing that even the promise of faith and forgiveness becomes a source of existential torment. Secret Sunshine &#8216;s Jeon Do-yeon won the Cannes Film Festival's best actress award for her powerful performance, a first for a Korean star.

Then came Poetry (2010), which continued Lee's fusion of generous, humanist drama with challenging philosophical inquiry, following an elderly woman who takes up writing poetry as she confronts the onset of Alzheimer's and her grandson's involvement in a horrific crime &#x2014; her search for beauty becoming inseparable from the obligation to face a truth she would rather not see. Lee won Cannes' best screenplay prize for the film. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid3' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

And Burning (2018), finally, transformed a short story by Haruki Murakami into a mesmerizing mystery of class resentment, thwarted desire and the existential angst of Korea's contemporary youth. It became the first South Korean film shortlisted for the foreign-language Oscar &#x2014; an embarrassingly late milestone, given how much indelible cinema the country had already produced. But the Academy quickly made up for lost time at the 2020 ceremony, when Parasite , directed by Lee's friend Bong Joon Ho, won four Oscars , including best picture.

Lee's legendary status at home also stems from the fact that his contribution to Korean cinema has gone well beyond his own filmmaking. In the 1990s, he became a leading campaigner for enforcement of the country's screen quota, which required theaters to show domestic movies for a minimum number of days each year. By protecting local filmmakers' access to audiences from Hollywood dominance, the policy created space for ambitious Korean cinema to flourish throughout the 2000s &#x2014; a driving force behind the broader Korean cultural wave still unfolding today. From 2003 to 2004, Lee even served as South Korea's minister of culture and tourism.

Over the summer, I flew to Seoul to meet the mercurial auteur Na Hong-jin at his office on the city's east side. A favorite of both action fans and art-house aficionados, Na studied under Lee as a graduate student at the prestigious Korea National University of Arts and later became an international cult sensation with a string of singularly twisted genre movies: the serial killer thriller The Chaser (2008), brutal crime flick The Yellow Sea (2010) and the supernatural freakout The Wailing (2016). blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid4' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

The occasion for our chat was the U.S. release of his divisive but wildly entertaining sci-fi action movie Hope . At the time, both his film and Lee's Possible Love were viewed as leading contenders to represent South Korea in the international Oscar race. ( Possible Love was ultimately selected and is now considered a frontrunner in the category.)

In what I thought would be a light, throwaway question, I asked how it felt to be competing with his former teacher. Na, who has a rather intense, skeptical bearing even when he's feeling friendly, now grew more serious, and perhaps more skeptical of his interlocutor.

&#8220;As a filmmaker, Lee Chang-dong is basically a god to me,&#8221; he said, raising his voice a little and staring at me implacably. &#8220;I don't compete with god, do you?&#8221;

Laughing at my startled reaction, he then softened his tone. &#8220;For those of us who came of age in the generations after him, Lee Chang-dong represents an older, in some ways better, era. His movies are difficult, but there's a tolerance and understanding to his work that I find almost healing. I don't know if this country still produces artists with that kind of depth.&#8221;

Lee's movie god never appeared on the set of Possible Love &#x2014; but the production was not without its moments of magic.

The film, which is currently streaming on Netflix globally, reunites Lee with Sul Kyung-gu, the unforgettable male lead of Peppermint Candy and Oasis , and Jeon of Secret Sunshine . Together, thanks in no small part to their performances in Lee's films, they are now regarded as screen royalty in Korea , having won seven Grand Bell Awards between them, their country's equivalent to the Oscars. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid5' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Sul plays Ho-seok, an autoworker left unemployed and profoundly damaged after a strike at his factory was violently suppressed by the state; Jeon is his loving, resourceful wife, Mi-ok, who works in a mask factory to keep their family afloat.

The drama begins to turn when their lives become unexpectedly entangled with those of an attractive couple from the opposite end of the class divide. Ye-ji (Cho Yeo-jeong, playing another rich wife as she famously did in Parasite ) is an aspiring documentary filmmaker working on a project about workers' rights in Korea, who becomes drawn to Ho-seok's story. Her husband, Sang-woo ( Hope 's Zo In-sung), is a dashing tycoon who seems happy to support her pursuits. The budding friendship among the foursome is warm, awkward and increasingly fraught, with Ye-ji's attempts at empathy growing difficult to disentangle from her eagerness to make art from the other couple's struggles.

The film culminates with a group trip to the coast. Hoping to convince Ho-seok to finally sit for a lengthy interview, Ye-ji invites the other couple to her husband's luxurious seaside family home for a weekend away. (Production designer Jang Min-woo and his team built the house from scratch to Lee's specifications.) Dinner on the first night becomes a messy drinking session. At dawn, Ho-seok staggers out toward the sea, and Mi-ok, curious about this exit, surreptitiously follows. The camera tracks them into a wind-tossed forest, where Ho-seok's suppressed rage fully erupts into a PTSD-driven breakdown: Screaming, he thrashes his body against the trees and branches around him, as his wife secretly watches in silence. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid6' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

As Mi-ok retreats in distress, she encounters Ye-ji in a clearing, attempting to film this painful private moment. The two women stare at each other from opposite edges of the frame as the wind howls and the trees sway behind them. This tableau, which captures so much of the story's complex meta-fictional pathos, later became one of Possible Love &#8216;s key marketing images.

Originally, in Lee's script, the sequence was supposed to take place on a calm, clear morning, says Kim, the cinematographer.

&#8220;But on the day of our tech scout to the coast, it was a really windy day, and director Lee and I both thought it looked great,&#8221; he recalls.

One of his country's most in-demand DPs, Kim is perhaps best known internationally for the baroque, highly stylized camerawork he employed on Park Chan-wook's romantic thriller, Decision to Leave (2022). He also shot the hit second and third seasons of Squid Game . For Possible Love , Kim set aside his impressionistic approach and strove instead for the most naturalistic look he could achieve, shooting nearly the entire film with a handheld camera he carried himself and relying on natural light as much as possible.

&#8220;Director Lee agreed it would add a nice layer if we could shoot it in a heavy wind,&#8221; Kim recalls. &#8220;And since we would be filming at the house for a month, I convinced him we could get all prepped, but then wait to see if we got lucky with a windy day or two.&#8221; blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid7' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

He adds: &#8220;And in my head, I thought, &#8216;Okay, this is where we'll meet the movie god, right?'&#8221;

&#8220;But then, that whole month: no wind. Not even a strong breeze.&#8221;

Although Lee is famous for the seriousness and solemnity with which he approaches his work, he is also widely admired in the Korean production world for the collaborative atmosphere on his sets.

Many leading auteurs readily discuss matters of craft but resist interpreting their work, preferring to leave its meaning to the audience. Lee believes it's vital that his collaborators understand the ideas and nuances each scene is intended to convey. His cinema may ask philosophical questions that are ultimately irresolvable, but he is resolutely clear and intentional about how his choices put those questions into play.

&#8220;If someone asks him something about a scene, no matter who it is on the crew &#x2014; maybe it's like an assistant set dresser &#x2014; he will stop and thoroughly explain the scene, his intentions, and how it fits into the whole movie,&#8221; Kim says. &#8220;I've worked on a lot of films, but I had never seen a director who is so patient and generous with his thoughts like that.&#8221;

So, when the winds never arrived for Possible Love &#8216;s poignant sunrise moment, Lee's team resolved to &#8220;create a crew god&#8221; for him instead.

&#8220;More than 100 people &#x2014; the assistants, some of the cast, and every member of the crew except for three or four of us huddled around the monitor with director Lee &#x2014; went into the forest and tied ropes to the trees and pulled like crazy so that the whole forest would sway like in a strong wind,&#8221; Kim recalls. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid8' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

He adds: &#8220;It wasn't anyone's job, but we all did it, because we knew how much that scene meant to him &#x2014; because he had told every one of us.&#8221;

Possible Love premiered at Venice in September, winning the festival's Silver Lion Grand Jury Prize. It later screened at the Toronto and New York film festivals, earning rave reviews at every stop. It currently has the highest Rotten Tomatoes score &#x2014; 97 percent fresh from 112 reviews &#x2014; of any major global release this year.

Possible Love was fully financed and produced by Netflix, and if the project seems a somewhat out-of-character choice for both Lee and the streamer (as more than a few critics have noted), it is also true that the film is the most critically acclaimed adult drama that the company has ever made &#x2014; holding higher average review scores than any of the other entries on Netflix's conspicuously short list of potential enduring classics ( The Irishman , Marriage Story , The Power of the Dog , All Quiet on the Western Front and Frankenstein all rank slightly lower, for example, while other lasting favorites, such as Alfonso Cuar&#xF3;n's Roma , were acquired but not produced by the streamer).

It is also the best-supported awards contender of Lee's long but still-too-overlooked career. Netflix is mounting a multi-front Oscar campaign extending beyond the international category to best picture, best director, best original screenplay and the acting categories, with a particularly ambitious push for Jeon .

Netflix had quietly been courting Lee since the company launched operations in South Korea a decade ago. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid9' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#8220;One of the top three rumors that's always floating around the Korean film industry is that Lee Chang-dong has finished a new script,&#8221; says Minyoung Kim, Netflix's top content executive in the Asia -Pacific region, who built the streamer's formidable Korean operations from the ground up.

In recent years, Lee's output has grown sporadic, with eight-year breaks between each of his last three features.

&#8220;The reason his films take so long is that he deeply, almost painfully, thinks about why a story needs to be told,&#8221; Kim explains. &#8220;Many directors are happy when they've found an entertaining idea that works, or a great piece of IP to adapt; with director Lee, it all starts with an observation about reality, or a question he can't resolve about life.&#8221;

She says she's aware of numerous potential projects &#x2014; all of which sounded, to her creative exec ear, like compelling, ready-to-go premises &#x2014; that Lee shelved because their themes didn't feel urgent enough.

&#8220;So, every time we heard those rumors about a new script over the years, we checked in &#x2014; just to be like, &#8216;We're here; we'd love to work with you!'&#8221;

&#8220;You could even say we were a little clingy,&#8221; Kim adds, laughing.

Netflix's opportunity to partner with Lee came after the auteur struggled to secure the relatively modest budget he was seeking for Possible Love &#x2014; reportedly around 8 billion won, or roughly $6 million &#x2014; after the key cast agreed to fee reductions to help him get the project made. South Korea's prolonged post-pandemic box-office slump has left its major studios increasingly cautious about backing serious dramas, even with filmmakers and casts of this stature. Kim believes Lee would eventually have found a local financier, but the studios' hesitancy gave Netflix its opening. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid10' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Netflix agreed to a larger, undisclosed budget and put its production resources behind the film. The commitment extended to a limited international theatrical release and substantial awards-season support, while leaving all creative decisions &#x2014; including final cut &#x2014; in Lee's hands.

&#8220;On a lot of our shows, we try to understand the director's vision, and become their thought partner. We debate and discuss, and try to make suggestions on what we believe will make the film stronger,&#8221; Netflix's Kim explains. Describing Lee as a maestro, she says, &#8220;he didn't need our opinions.&#8221;

She adds: &#8220;We just tried to understand his vision as fully as we could, so we could support him at every step of the way.&#8221;

The Hollywood Reporter recently spoke with Lee in Seoul about the creation of Possible Love and the ideas behind several pivotal scenes. The conversation includes spoilers.

From what I understand, Possible Love began more than a decade ago as a project about the workers involved in Korea's real-life SsangYong Motor strike, which resembles the episode depicted in the film. You wrote a script and went into pre-production, then shelved it. What concerned you about that earlier version?

It's true that the project began for me with the 2009 SsangYong Motor incident, but when I speak about the origins of the story today, I try not to mention the company or the specific year, because I don't want too much emphasis placed on that particular incident. There have been many similar cases in the Korean labor movement, and I'd like the film to be viewed in that broader context. But yes, about 10 years ago, that case is what got me started. I did a lot of research, wrote a screenplay and went into preproduction. And then I shelved it &#x2014; for two reasons. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid11' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

First, I was constantly asking myself: What meaning does it have for me to turn this episode into a dramatic feature? What does it mean to me personally? What will it mean to the audience? And what meaning will it hold as a film in itself? I just couldn't find answers I had real conviction in. There was already a documentary about the strike and the lives of the workers involved, and I felt it was a very honest and thorough piece of work. [Lee is referring to Seo Seh-chin's 2009 documentary Before the Full Moon .] Ultimately, I didn't see how I could add to what the documentary had already achieved simply by turning it into a dramatic feature.

The second factor was connected to feelings and beliefs I have long held as a creator. I was writing fiction back in the 1970s and 1980s, often about Korean society and the issues within it. Naturally, I wrote about my times, and because of what our country was going through, that meant writing about people who were suffering &#x2014; people living under our authoritarian military government, factory workers facing hardship, students participating in the democracy movement and those hurting because of our divided nation.

And I always ended up asking myself, &#8220;Am I worthy of telling this story? Does it hold meaning for me to be the one saying this?&#8221; I asked these questions because my life and my situation were often very different from those of the people I was portraying. I wasn't suffering as badly as they were. So what right did I have? blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid12' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

As I worked on the early versions of Possible Love , all these questions came back to me, and I couldn't find conviction in any approach. So I shelved it.

Then, as the years passed, the issue of labor itself began to go through big changes. At the time of the strike, the focus was on the direct pain the laid-off workers were suffering as we entered a more global, capital-driven system &#x2014; the suffering of those betrayed and abandoned. Now, that pain is becoming universal. The issue today is that labor itself is on the verge of disappearing. There is no longer as clear a line between the working class and the nonworking class, because we are beginning to see the disappearance of labor itself, and all the definitions of self that come with it. These shifts got me interested again, but I was still finding it difficult to move forward.

It was only when my co-writer, Oh Jung-mi, suggested placing a documentary filmmaker within the story that I began to see a path with some legitimacy. Now it was also my story, as the creator and teller of other people's stories. It allowed me to question my own position: What are my attitudes? What's built into the gaze I direct toward these people? And what should my ethics be?

That must have been such an exciting discovery &#x2014; or simply a relief. With Ye-ji as your avatar, you seem able to express your best intentions as a storyteller while voicing your bitterly ironic self-doubts and unresolved questions about such storytelling. As a viewer, it feels like an ongoing, self-aware conversation with you. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid13' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

You could also say that I gave myself an alibi. But this approach really did free me to tell their story without those earlier questions constantly tugging at my heart. It put me a little more at ease about telling the story of this laid-off worker and his family.

Strictly speaking, is Ye-ji my complete embodiment, my true avatar? Of course not. She and I are very different. But from a larger perspective, you could say that Ye-ji really does embody my best intentions and represents exactly who I am within the structure of this story. I felt this very keenly all over again at the world premiere in Venice. There I was in a very expensive rented tuxedo, walking a red carpet to present the story of a laid-off worker's pain. I suppose some would say that is simply the nature of things, because filmmaking is married to capital.

Throughout the film, there are moments where characters seem to give direct voice to your self-conscious misgivings, as we've been discussing. Other questions are suggested by the plot, but aren't stated quite so explicitly. One is related to the issue you just mentioned: Does art carry any real power to critique or change the capitalist system, or is the aesthetic itself just another part of the system's healthy functioning? You certainly seem to be nudging us in the direction of the latter. Ye-ji, the film's well-intentioned artist, after all, is married to and supported by a capitalist tycoon. The documentary she is making seems entirely genuine &#x2014; but the fact remains that her real-life husband is executing a private equity deal to sell off the factory at the heart of her story the entire time. By extension, this feels not dissimilar to the fact that increasingly large swaths of the global movie business are owned by some of the very same tech giants and oligarchs that are driving the technological changes that will potentially bring about the elimination of labor you mentioned earlier. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid14' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

This question goes to the very essence of the film industry, and art-making in general &#x2014; and who in this world could give a clear answer to that, right? That said, I have never felt free of this question since the very beginning of my work as a filmmaker. It's always there.

One answer, for me at least, is that I believe films must never turn a blind eye to the real world and the people living within it. By "turn a blind eye," I mean that the lives you portray in your film should never be consumed simply as material or content for making your movie.

Of course, we're talking about a very fine line. When you are dealing with people in pain &#x2014; people suffering from the many paradoxes of life &#x2014; you must ask yourself: Am I only consuming or exploiting their story, or am I really trying to start a conversation with the audience in a meaningful and creative way? That's a very difficult question to answer with full conviction.

As a rule of thumb, I believe that the simpler the objective of your story becomes, the further you have probably drifted from reality. I believe that a film exists to ask questions of the audience. If your audience leaves the theater having only experienced catharsis &#x2014; entertainment or pleasure &#x2014; then you have probably treated your subject as little more than an exercise in consumption.

Let's talk about the film's dual climaxes at the vacation home. How should we interpret Mi-ok's actions on the beach? I read them as both an attempt to experience the freedom Sang-woo so effortlessly enjoys and a way to reclaim some power over him. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-thr-article-mid-articleX-uid15' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setLazyLoadMultiplier(2) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

