George Bernard Shaw famously resisted a romantic ending for &#8220;Pygmalion,&#8221; the 1913 play the musical is based on. Friedman acknowledges its influence but says she has to make the musical's script work on its own terms, steering consciously away from &#8220;any point of ever thinking that there's a love affair. It's not going to happen.&#8221;

Nor is anyone sanding down Higgins. Cumming calls it a period piece about the patriarchy, in which a strong woman comes in and tries to bash it, and says he is serving both the musical and Shaw's play. &#8220;I'm not interested in flattening things out to make them uncontentious,&#8221; he says. &#8220;I am interested in finding a way to play people so that you understand them.&#8221;

